1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,530
Imagine joining a multiplayer
game for the first time.

2
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:08,450
In games like World of Warcraft,
Final Fantasy XIV or The Elder

3
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:12,280
Scrolls Online, groups are
built around three basic roles.

4
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:16,330
You can deal damage. You
can heal your teammates.

5
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:18,120
Or you can become the tank.

6
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:21,450
The person who stands
directly in front of the thing

7
00:00:21,530 --> 00:00:25,300
everyone else is trying very
hard not to stand in front of.

8
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:29,710
And of the three, the tank is
often the one nobody wants to be.

9
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:35,470
Which is a little odd. Because
apparently, this is a very important job.

10
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:38,120
The boss is attacking you.

11
00:00:38,770 --> 00:00:40,440
Your teammates are depending on you.

12
00:00:41,152 --> 00:00:43,382
If you make a mistake, someone might die.

13
00:00:43,832 --> 00:00:46,022
If you die, everyone might die.

14
00:00:46,912 --> 00:00:49,262
And if the group wipes? Well...

15
00:00:49,552 --> 00:00:51,442
Everyone knows whose fault it is.

16
00:00:52,202 --> 00:00:55,172
So you might expect tanking
to be incredibly difficult.

17
00:00:56,362 --> 00:00:58,882
But here's the strange thing. Sometimes...

18
00:00:58,962 --> 00:01:00,612
it really isn't.

19
00:01:03,022 --> 00:01:04,972
Let's look at a typical boss fight.

20
00:01:05,822 --> 00:01:07,252
The damage dealers are busy.

21
00:01:07,902 --> 00:01:11,952
They have rotations to
maintain. Abilities to time.

22
00:01:12,712 --> 00:01:13,652
Resources to manage.

23
00:01:13,732 --> 00:01:16,302
Buffs and debuffs to track.

24
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:19,611
They need to stay out of dangerous areas.

25
00:01:20,341 --> 00:01:23,711
And they need to do all of that while
watching the boss for the next mechanic.

26
00:01:24,911 --> 00:01:26,711
The healer has their own problems.

27
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:30,001
They need to keep everyone
alive. Watch health bars.

28
00:01:30,959 --> 00:01:31,959
Manage their resources.

29
00:01:32,039 --> 00:01:35,709
And anticipate when the
next big hit is coming.

30
00:01:36,769 --> 00:01:38,119
And then there's the tank.

31
00:01:40,419 --> 00:01:44,609
The tank walks towards the boss.
Uses a taunt. And... stands there.

32
00:01:45,315 --> 00:01:46,515
That's it.

33
00:01:48,453 --> 00:01:49,703
Well, not exactly.

34
00:01:50,743 --> 00:01:53,673
The tank needs to keep the boss
pointed away from the group.

35
00:01:54,543 --> 00:01:55,743
Block the big attacks.

36
00:01:56,633 --> 00:01:58,003
Move when necessary.

37
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:00,643
Keep the boss where the group wants it.

38
00:02:01,093 --> 00:02:02,723
And occasionally deal with some adds.

39
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:05,930
But once everything is under control...

40
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:09,310
there can be an astonishing
amount of nothing happening.

41
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:11,930
The boss hits you. You block.

42
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,590
The boss hits you again. You block again.

43
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:19,080
Your abilities come off cooldown.
You press them. And then... you wait.

44
00:02:19,886 --> 00:02:22,286
Meanwhile, your damage
dealers are performing what

45
00:02:22,366 --> 00:02:25,196
looks like a small piano
recital on their keyboards.

46
00:02:26,626 --> 00:02:29,516
Now imagine one of your damage
dealers makes a mistake.

47
00:02:30,426 --> 00:02:33,676
They stand in something
they shouldn't. They die.

48
00:02:34,716 --> 00:02:35,946
What does the tank do?

49
00:02:36,776 --> 00:02:38,106
Usually... nothing.

50
00:02:40,669 --> 00:02:42,499
The boss is still attacking the tank.

51
00:02:43,169 --> 00:02:44,659
The healer can resurrect them.

52
00:02:45,189 --> 00:02:46,579
The group can continue.

53
00:02:47,509 --> 00:02:50,729
In fact, a surprisingly
large part of being a tank is

54
00:02:50,809 --> 00:02:53,499
accepting that other people
are going to make mistakes.

55
00:02:54,839 --> 00:02:57,009
And that's not necessarily your problem.

56
00:02:57,659 --> 00:02:59,609
A dead damage dealer is unfortunate.

57
00:03:00,979 --> 00:03:03,549
A dead tank is a completely
different situation.

58
00:03:04,659 --> 00:03:08,089
Because the boss immediately
finds somebody who cannot take it.

59
00:03:09,199 --> 00:03:11,329
And that's when things become interesting.

60
00:03:13,979 --> 00:03:18,749
The tank isn't really there to keep
everyone alive. That's the healer's job.

61
00:03:19,559 --> 00:03:21,909
The tank's job is to
control the encounter.

62
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:24,869
The boss should be looking at you.

63
00:03:25,339 --> 00:03:27,509
The boss should be
standing where you want it.

64
00:03:28,459 --> 00:03:32,029
Its dangerous attacks should be
directed away from everyone else.

65
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:35,869
And when the boss does something
particularly unpleasant...

66
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:38,709
you should be the one
standing there saying:

67
00:03:39,468 --> 00:03:40,778
"Whenever you are ready."

68
00:03:43,278 --> 00:03:46,168
This is why tanking can
feel strangely relaxing.

69
00:03:47,098 --> 00:03:49,508
You're not trying to kill the
boss as quickly as possible.

70
00:03:50,708 --> 00:03:52,658
You're trying to make
the fight predictable.

71
00:03:53,448 --> 00:03:56,018
And predictable fights are
much easier to survive.

72
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:59,288
A role can be extremely important without

73
00:03:59,368 --> 00:04:02,598
requiring you to constantly
do complicated things.

74
00:04:05,438 --> 00:04:07,468
Imagine a machine with ten components.

75
00:04:08,238 --> 00:04:10,748
Nine of them are doing
something all the time.

76
00:04:11,378 --> 00:04:13,048
One is mostly sitting there.

77
00:04:13,748 --> 00:04:15,658
But if that one component fails...

78
00:04:15,868 --> 00:04:17,598
the whole machine stops.

79
00:04:18,438 --> 00:04:20,888
That doesn't mean the
component is complicated.

80
00:04:22,148 --> 00:04:23,378
It means it's critical.

81
00:04:26,038 --> 00:04:27,618
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

82
00:04:27,698 --> 00:04:30,448
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

83
00:04:31,298 --> 00:04:32,768
It really helps the channel.

84
00:04:33,758 --> 00:04:36,208
Tanking often works exactly like this.

85
00:04:36,858 --> 00:04:39,868
The tank may have fewer things
to do than the damage dealers.

86
00:04:41,018 --> 00:04:43,508
But some of those things
have enormous consequences.

87
00:04:44,908 --> 00:04:46,638
Press the wrong button as a DPS?

88
00:04:47,208 --> 00:04:49,298
Maybe you lose some damage.

89
00:04:50,098 --> 00:04:52,178
Miss a tank mechanic at the wrong moment?

90
00:04:52,258 --> 00:04:53,948
The boss may kill you.

91
00:04:54,638 --> 00:04:56,688
And now the entire group has a problem.

92
00:04:57,298 --> 00:04:59,048
Unless there is a second tank.

93
00:05:00,038 --> 00:05:02,748
And still, hardly anyone
puts their hand up.

94
00:05:03,748 --> 00:05:04,948
Games have noticed.

95
00:05:05,928 --> 00:05:09,368
World of Warcraft once
added Call to Arms: a bag of

96
00:05:09,448 --> 00:05:12,478
extra rewards for whichever
role the queue was short of.

97
00:05:13,498 --> 00:05:19,828
Final Fantasy XIV has its own version, and
it even has a name. Adventurer in Need.

98
00:05:20,808 --> 00:05:22,928
Entire reward systems, built to bribe

99
00:05:23,008 --> 00:05:26,038
somebody into a role
nobody volunteered for.

100
00:05:28,478 --> 00:05:31,808
So if tanking can be this simple,
why does it feel so intimidating?

101
00:05:33,288 --> 00:05:38,478
When you're a damage dealer, you can often
make a mistake quietly. Your damage drops.

102
00:05:39,248 --> 00:05:41,058
Maybe nobody even notices.

103
00:05:42,048 --> 00:05:44,358
But when you're the tank,
your mistakes are visible.

104
00:05:45,248 --> 00:05:46,978
The boss suddenly turns around.

105
00:05:47,408 --> 00:05:48,758
Someone gets hit.

106
00:05:49,508 --> 00:05:53,598
The group wipes. And you immediately
know: "Yeah. That was me."

107
00:05:54,388 --> 00:05:56,568
That's enough to make a
perfectly competent player

108
00:05:56,648 --> 00:05:59,738
think: "I don't want to
be responsible for this."

109
00:06:00,778 --> 00:06:02,708
You don't actually need to be perfect.

110
00:06:03,818 --> 00:06:05,048
You need to be reliable.

111
00:06:07,558 --> 00:06:10,578
A tank who does five things
consistently is worth more

112
00:06:10,658 --> 00:06:13,488
than one who knows twenty
tricks and forgets the basics.

113
00:06:14,708 --> 00:06:17,978
And there's another reason tanking
is more forgiving than it looks.

114
00:06:18,848 --> 00:06:20,738
A tank is usually built to survive.

115
00:06:21,848 --> 00:06:23,828
Lots of health. High resistances.

116
00:06:23,908 --> 00:06:26,978
Damage mitigation. Shields. Blocking.

117
00:06:27,758 --> 00:06:30,668
The whole character is designed
to be difficult to kill.

118
00:06:31,478 --> 00:06:33,908
Damage dealers are often
the exact opposite.

119
00:06:35,148 --> 00:06:37,458
Because DDs are usually glass cannons.

120
00:06:40,238 --> 00:06:44,278
They're built to turn as much of their
survivability as possible into damage.

121
00:06:44,358 --> 00:06:46,928
And that creates a funny situation.

122
00:06:48,308 --> 00:06:50,818
A tank can take a bad
hit and lose some health.

123
00:06:51,128 --> 00:06:53,238
A damage dealer takes the same hit...

124
00:06:54,418 --> 00:06:55,988
and suddenly they're a corpse.

125
00:06:56,978 --> 00:06:59,178
So while the tank may have
more responsibility for

126
00:06:59,258 --> 00:07:02,608
the encounter, the tank often
has more room for error.

127
00:07:03,818 --> 00:07:05,788
Which is probably not what you'd expect.

128
00:07:06,058 --> 00:07:08,128
The role that looks the most dangerous...

129
00:07:09,378 --> 00:07:11,488
may actually be one of the most forgiving.

130
00:07:14,488 --> 00:07:16,038
But there is a catch to it.

131
00:07:16,818 --> 00:07:19,668
Everything I've described
so far works beautifully...

132
00:07:20,138 --> 00:07:22,428
until the encounter becomes difficult.

133
00:07:23,578 --> 00:07:26,388
Now the tank may have to
position the boss precisely.

134
00:07:27,688 --> 00:07:29,348
Move it at specific moments.

135
00:07:29,428 --> 00:07:31,858
Control additional enemies.

136
00:07:32,248 --> 00:07:35,878
Interrupt abilities. Manage
resources. Swap weapons.

137
00:07:36,688 --> 00:07:40,058
Swap with the other tank before
the debuffs stack up and kill you.

138
00:07:40,528 --> 00:07:43,518
Use defensive abilities
before the damage arrives.

139
00:07:44,598 --> 00:07:48,248
And sometimes perform mechanics
that nobody else in the group can.

140
00:07:49,408 --> 00:07:51,538
Now the tank isn't just standing there.

141
00:07:52,418 --> 00:07:53,648
The tank is planning.

142
00:07:55,928 --> 00:07:57,898
And this is where tanking changes.

143
00:07:58,748 --> 00:08:01,338
You're no longer reacting
to what the boss just did.

144
00:08:01,808 --> 00:08:04,618
You're thinking about what
the boss is going to do next.

145
00:08:05,628 --> 00:08:08,698
So maybe we can think about
tanking as three different jobs.

146
00:08:09,938 --> 00:08:13,088
At the first level: "Don't let
the boss attack everyone else."

147
00:08:13,858 --> 00:08:15,348
That's surprisingly easy.

148
00:08:16,348 --> 00:08:18,358
At the second: "Control the battlefield."

149
00:08:19,488 --> 00:08:21,568
Now you're positioning
enemies, managing adds

150
00:08:21,648 --> 00:08:23,878
and helping the group deal damage safely.

151
00:08:25,098 --> 00:08:28,408
And at the highest level:
"Understand the entire encounter."

152
00:08:29,708 --> 00:08:33,178
You know what's coming. You
know where the boss needs to be.

153
00:08:33,608 --> 00:08:35,438
You know when the group needs your help.

154
00:08:36,418 --> 00:08:38,628
And you're effectively
conducting the fight.

155
00:08:39,438 --> 00:08:41,198
The first level doesn't
have to be terrifying

156
00:08:41,278 --> 00:08:43,668
just because the third one is difficult.

157
00:08:44,908 --> 00:08:47,998
You don't learn to drive by
entering a Formula One race.

158
00:08:49,218 --> 00:08:51,178
And you don't need to
tank the hardest raid in

159
00:08:51,258 --> 00:08:53,928
the game to find out
whether you enjoy tanking.

160
00:08:56,528 --> 00:09:00,598
There is also something tanking gives
you that damage dealing often doesn't.

161
00:09:01,738 --> 00:09:02,938
Time.

162
00:09:05,527 --> 00:09:07,237
More time to look at the encounter.

163
00:09:07,987 --> 00:09:09,857
More time to observe the boss.

164
00:09:10,647 --> 00:09:12,717
More time to understand what's happening.

165
00:09:13,737 --> 00:09:17,367
You're not squeezing every last
percent of performance out of yourself.

166
00:09:17,897 --> 00:09:19,787
You're trying to maintain control.

167
00:09:20,937 --> 00:09:22,927
And once you've achieved that control...

168
00:09:23,037 --> 00:09:25,547
you can sometimes just stand there.

169
00:09:26,307 --> 00:09:27,867
The boss hits you. You block.

170
00:09:27,947 --> 00:09:29,397
Your teammates do their thing.

171
00:09:30,137 --> 00:09:32,747
And you think: "Wait.
Is this really my job?"

172
00:09:33,597 --> 00:09:35,087
Yes. That is the job.

173
00:09:37,737 --> 00:09:39,857
We often assume that the
most important person

174
00:09:39,937 --> 00:09:42,767
in a team must also be the busiest person.

175
00:09:43,437 --> 00:09:45,367
But that's not necessarily true.

176
00:09:46,407 --> 00:09:48,687
Sometimes the most valuable
thing you can do is

177
00:09:48,767 --> 00:09:51,697
make sure one very important
thing doesn't go wrong.

178
00:09:52,667 --> 00:09:54,637
The tank doesn't have to kill the boss.

179
00:09:55,367 --> 00:09:57,217
The tank doesn't have to heal everyone.

180
00:09:58,027 --> 00:10:00,817
The tank doesn't even have
to prevent every mistake.

181
00:10:02,087 --> 00:10:06,817
The tank simply has to make the fight
manageable. Predictable. Controlled.

182
00:10:07,927 --> 00:10:09,777
And when everything goes well...

183
00:10:10,427 --> 00:10:12,857
it can look like the tank
is doing almost nothing.

184
00:10:13,207 --> 00:10:15,557
Which is perhaps the ultimate compliment.

185
00:10:16,587 --> 00:10:19,157
Because when the tank is
doing their job perfectly...

186
00:10:19,347 --> 00:10:21,917
the rest of the group gets to do theirs.

187
00:10:22,927 --> 00:10:26,057
And maybe that's why tanking
isn't as scary as it looks.

188
00:10:26,807 --> 00:10:28,617
You aren't carrying the entire group.

189
00:10:29,517 --> 00:10:33,207
You're just standing in exactly the place
where the boss wants to kill someone.

190
00:10:34,327 --> 00:10:35,997
And making sure... it's you.
